<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Gambia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gambia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gambia is a small country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> along the Gambia River. The region formed part of the sphere of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> state that dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a> region for several centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-gambia.jpg" alt="The Gambia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vzb83 iThe source code of the previous SVG was invalid due to 39 errors. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_The_Gambia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gambia is the smallest country on the African mainland. Its borders mirror the meandering Gambia River, which flows through the middle of the country, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> surrounds it on all sides except the 80 kilometres of Atlantic coastline in the west. It is less than 50 kilometres wide at its widest point, with an area of 11,295 square kilometres. The present boundaries were defined in 1889 after an agreement between the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, though fixing the final borders took nearly 15 years. The river, known locally as the River, is navigable deep into the continent.</p><p>The name derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> term Kambra or Kambaa, meaning the Gambia River. Portuguese explorers adopted a variation of it, which was later anglicised. In 1964 Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawda-jawara">Dawda Jawara</a> asked that the name keep the definite article, in part to reduce confusion with the newly independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Arab traders gave the first written accounts of the area in the ninth and tenth centuries. Muslim merchants and scholars established communities in West African commercial centres and carried out trans-Saharan trade, exporting gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and people taken captive in raids and sold as slaves. At the beginning of the 14th century most of the present Gambia was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. The Portuguese reached the area by sea in the mid-15th century and began to dominate overseas trade.</p><p>In 1588 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-prior-of-crato">António, Prior of Crato</a>, a claimant to the Portuguese throne, sold exclusive trade rights on the river to English merchants, and in 1618 King James I granted a charter to an English company. Between 1651 and 1661 parts of the Gambia, including St. Andrew's Island and Fort Jakob, were ruled by the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler">Jacob Kettler</a>, before being ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1664.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> struggled for supremacy on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and Gambia rivers through the 18th century. The 1783 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> gave Britain the Gambia River, but France kept an enclave at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albreda">Albreda</a> until it was ceded in 1856. The British established the military post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Bathurst</a>, now Banjul, in 1816, and the Gambia gained independence on 18 February 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Gambia is the Republic of The Gambia, a republic since 1970, and its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Banjul</a>, an island city once called Bathurst. English is the sole official language, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> is spoken as a first language by 38 per cent of the population. The Mandinka are the most numerous ethnic group. The river and its slave-trade history are the setting of Alex Haley's Roots.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Gambia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…towns such as Sinsani (Sansanding) and Nyamina, bought captives and grain and dealt in Saharan salt, cloth, gold, horses and firearms, and many captives from the middle Niger were sold on to the French and British posts on the Senegal and the <b>Gambia</b> and shipped across the Atlantic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kaabu Empire began in the thirteenth century as a western province of the Mali Empire, in the region corresponding to present-day Guinea-Bissau, southern Senegal and <b>The Gambia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The persecution of the Rohingya led <b>The Gambia</b> to bring a genocide case against Myanmar at the International Court of Justice in 2019 and created one of the world's largest refugee camps, at Cox's Bazar in Bangladesh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It has also become part of the official national mythology of Mali, the <b>Gambia</b>, Senegal and Guinea, and is studied in history lessons in their primary schools.</blockquote><a href="/songs/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1821, after the dissolution of the Company of Merchants, Britain placed its forts on the Gold Coast and its post on the <b>Gambia</b> under the governor of Sierra Leone, Charles MacCarthy, as the British West African Settlements.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-west-africa">British West Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Jacob Kettler of the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia, who built a merchant fleet, acquired St Andrew's Island in the <b>Gambia</b> River from local rulers in 1651.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-gambia">Courland Gambia</a></li></ul></section>
